import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Figure files: plots written with png() and dev.off() are figures","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"records/figure-files-episode.md","filePath":"records/figure-files-episode.md"}'),i={name:"records/figure-files-episode.md"};function n(r,e,d,h,l,c){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Historical record</p><p>This page records what happened on a date, with its observed failures and limits. It is not a requirement for the current implementation. Current behaviour is in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a>.</p></div><h1 id="figure-files-plots-written-with-png-and-dev-off-are-figures" tabindex="-1">Figure files: plots written with png() and dev.off() are figures <a class="header-anchor" href="#figure-files-plots-written-with-png-and-dev-off-are-figures" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Figure files: plots written with png() and dev.off() are figures&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Date: 2026-11-08. Stage 5 of the built-in <a href="/Rho/docs/records/figures-episode">Figures</a> capability, after <a href="/Rho/docs/records/python-figures-episode">Python figures</a>, observed in one real Agent episode run three times. This is functional evidence, not a usability claim: no independent researcher has used it.</p><h2 id="why-it-changed" tabindex="-1">Why it changed <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-it-changed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why it changed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In both runs of <a href="/Rho/docs/records/drawn-figures-episode">Drawn figures</a>, Claude Code saved the MA plot and the heatmap for the report the way R scripts do: <code>png(&quot;figures/ma_plot.png&quot;)</code>, <code>plotMA(res)</code>, <code>dev.off()</code>. Those files stayed pictures, and the page&#39;s <code>figure.read</code> for them was answered 404. Only <code>ggsave()</code> named its file to the describer: its frame is on the call stack when its page starts. A device the code opens itself leaves no such trace, and a file device keeps no record of what it drew.</p><h2 id="what-runs-now" tabindex="-1">What runs now <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-runs-now" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What runs now&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Files the code opens.</strong> The R runner (Notebook 0.5.12) traces <code>png()</code>, <code>jpeg()</code>, <code>tiff()</code>, <code>bmp()</code>, <code>svg()</code>, <code>pdf()</code>, <code>cairo_pdf()</code> and <code>dev.off()</code> in grDevices, so it knows each device the code opens on a file and the file&#39;s name. It turns on the device&#39;s display list when a page starts and records the page before the next page starts, after each top-level expression and before <code>dev.off()</code> closes it. When the cell ends, the last page of each closed file is described at the device&#39;s size, like a ggsave page: from its recording (base graphics), from the frame of <code>draw()</code> (a ComplexHeatmap) or from the ggplot <code>print()</code> drew. A name numbered with <code>%d</code> is a figure for each page; a PDF of several pages says why it is not described. A device left open stays the session&#39;s current device in the next cell, as in the R console. A page no describer knows (lattice) stays a picture. <code>png</code> now prints as a traced function.</li><li><strong>Marks.</strong> A figure binds up to 50,000 marks in R and Python, up from 30,000.</li><li><strong>Computed positions in base graphics.</strong> Points, lines and labels drawn through <code>-log10</code>, <code>log10</code>, <code>log2</code>, <code>log2(x + 1)</code> or <code>log1p</code> of a column are bound through it, as in Python. When an earlier layer of the same plot draws the same column on the same axis through another transform, the later layer&#39;s note says so; the Python describer does too since Figures 0.5.1. A value that is not finite matches only itself.</li><li><strong>File figures on open pages.</strong> The answer board and the file view pass the page&#39;s event cursor to the figure (Figures 0.5.0). A file whose bytes no cell has named yet is asked again when the page&#39;s events move on. In a figure narrower than its tooltip, the tooltip spans the figure.</li></ul><h2 id="the-episode" tabindex="-1">The episode <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-episode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The episode&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An owned service with a disposable project, <code>airway-files</code>, ran in Chrome at 1440 × 1000 and 390 × 844. The project started empty; R 4.5.2 had DESeq2 1.48.2, ComplexHeatmap 2.26.0 and the <code>airway</code> package. Real Claude Code 2.1.319 ran from the Agent panel in its default mode. The person asked: <em>Fit DESeq2 to the airway data (dexamethasone-treated vs untreated airway cells). In the Notebook, draw the MA plot with plotMA and a ComplexHeatmap of the 30 most significant genes (scaled variance-stabilised expression, rows labelled with gene symbols, samples annotated by treatment), and save both as PNG files in figures/ for my report. Then tell me what stands out.</em></p><p>Westlake HPC&#39;s route for DeepSeek V4.1 Flash still refused the third request of a three-step <code>claude -p</code> (<em>400 WestlakeHPC: Duplicate item found with id .</em>), as in <a href="/Rho/docs/records/python-figures-episode#the-episode">Python figures</a>. All three runs used the same model through <code>workbuddy-ai/deepseek-v4.1-flash-sg</code>, which completed that check.</p><p><strong>Run 1.</strong> Claude Code answered in 3 min. Its cells wrote <code>figures/ma_plot.png</code>, <code>figures/ma_plot_unshrunken.png</code> and <code>figures/top30_heatmap.png</code> with <code>png()</code> and <code>dev.off()</code>. The runner found all three files. The heatmap file was a figure, and both MA plots were refused: <em>The plot draws more than the 30000 marks a figure binds.</em> <code>plotMA</code> drew every gene with a count, 33,469 of them; the Drawn figures runs had filtered first. With the limit lifted, describing the shrunken MA plot took 2.5 s and 8.5 MB, and all 33,469 points were bound to <code>res_shrunk</code>. The limit is now 50,000 marks; a record over 16 MiB is still refused with its size.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/ma-refused.png" alt="Run 1: the saved MA plot refused at 30,000 marks"></p><p><strong>Run 2</strong>, with the higher limit, answered in 5 min. Its cells saved <code>figures/ma_plot.png</code> twice (20,227 marks) and <code>figures/heatmap_top30.png</code> twice (240 cells), each a figure of the bytes it wrote. The MA plot was wrong. To colour the significant genes, Claude Code drew them again over <code>plotMA</code> at <code>log2(res$baseMean)</code>, on an axis that <code>plotMA</code> draws as <code>baseMean</code> on a log scale. All 4,081 landed at x = 3 to 18 on that axis, at the low-count end, while the answer called the plot an MA plot with the significant points overlaid. The figure showed exactly what the file did, but the 4,081 overlay points were not bound: the R describer matched only columns drawn as they are.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/ma-overlay-file.png" alt="Run 2: the file Claude Code saved, its significant genes drawn at log2 of their mean"></p><p>Replayed with R matching through transforms, the overlay was bound to <code>res</code> as <code>x: log2(baseMean)</code>, with the note <em>Its x is log2(baseMean), while layer 0 of this plot draws x as baseMean on the same axis.</em> <code>plot(res$log2FoldChange, -log10(res$padj))</code> was bound as <code>y: -log10(padj)</code>. The replay&#39;s overlaid MA plot (about 26,400 marks, filtered differently) took 3.4 s to describe; run 2&#39;s own record of 20,227 marks, matched without transforms, took 1.7 s.</p><p>During run 2 the page asked <code>figure.read</code> for <code>ma_plot.png</code> with bytes a cell had saved and was answered 404. Run 3 showed why.</p><p><strong>Run 3</strong>, after these changes, answered in 3.5 min. Its cells saved <code>figures/ma_plot.png</code> and <code>figures/ma_plot_shrunken.png</code> (16,142 marks each) and <code>figures/heatmap_top30.png</code> (240 cells). Claude Code then ran its script <code>scripts/deseq_airway.R</code> with <code>Rscript</code> from a shell, which wrote the same bytes, so the files stayed figures of the cells. The open page again logged 404s for both MA plots, with the bytes the cell had saved.</p><p>The board learns of a file when <code>dev.off()</code> writes it, and the cell names its bytes only when it ends, after describing the page. A figure view that asked in between kept the picture. A cell that wrote <code>figures/race.png</code> and then slept 8 s reproduced it with the file view open. With the previous view the file stayed a picture for the 25 s watched; with the new view it was a picture from 3 s and a figure at 12 s, once the cell ended.</p><p>In the file view, pointing at the highest point of <code>ma_plot.png</code> read its row of <code>res</code>. Its hint carried the layer&#39;s note: <em>3 of 16139 marks are drawn at a y other than their log2FoldChange</em>, the points clipped at the plot&#39;s limit.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/ma-file-hover.png" alt="The saved MA plot read point by point in the file view"></p><p>The person dragged over the well-expressed genes that go up most (10 points, <em>rows of res</em>) and asked: <em>These are the well-expressed genes that go up most. Which are they, and are any of them in the heatmap too? Mark the ones in the heatmap.</em> The message carried <em>10 points · ma_plot.png</em>. Claude Code answered in 97 s. It named the ten genes and said five are in the heatmap (SAMHD1, GPX3, MAOA, DUSP1, CCDC69), because the heatmap ranks by adjusted p, not fold change. It marked the five on the file.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/ma-file-selected.png" alt="The person&#39;s selection on the saved MA plot"></p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/ma-file-marks.png" alt="Claude Code&#39;s five marks on the same file"></p><p>On <code>heatmap_top30.png</code> the person dragged over the down-regulated slice (64 cells, <em>rows of mat</em>) and asked: <em>These genes go down with dexamethasone. Is the drop the same in every cell line? Point at the samples where it is weakest.</em> Claude Code answered in 3 min. It said the drop is about the same in every line (no gene&#39;s difference between lines below an adjusted p of 0.32), with N080611 the weakest for VCAM1, KCTD12 and SOX4. It marked 16 cells: the untreated and treated sample of each gene&#39;s weakest line.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/heat-file-selected.png" alt="The down-regulated slice selected on the saved heatmap"></p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/heat-file-marks.png" alt="Claude Code&#39;s 16 cells on the same file"></p><p>The person then drew run 2&#39;s overlay code in a cell of this session. The note showed under the figure. They dragged over the cluster at the left edge (995 marks) and asked: <em>Why are almost all the significant genes at the low-count end of this MA plot? Is that right?</em> Claude Code answered in 109 s: <em>No, that isn&#39;t right. The pile-up comes from a plotting bug in cell 15, not from the data.</em> It said 882 of the 995 marks are the misplaced points. It showed that the share of significant genes rises with expression, from 9 % below a mean of 50 to 44 % above 5,000. It drew the corrected plot in a cell. Its <code>figure.read</code> result carried the overlay&#39;s mapping and the note, as well as the cell&#39;s code.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/overlay-note.png" alt="Run 2&#39;s overlay drawn again, with its note"></p><p>At 390 px, pointing at a heatmap cell near the figure&#39;s left edge put the tooltip outside the figure, its values wrapped one character per line over the cells. The tooltip now spans the figure when neither side of the pointer holds it.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/narrow-tip.png" alt="The tooltip at 390 px"></p><h2 id="iterations-and-causes" tabindex="-1">Iterations and causes <a class="header-anchor" href="#iterations-and-causes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Iterations and causes&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Saved MA plots refused.</strong> <code>plotMA</code> draws every gene with a count. 33,469 points took 2.5 s and 8.5 MB; the limit is 50,000 marks.</li><li><strong>A misplaced overlay was unbound.</strong> R matched only columns drawn as they are. It now matches through the transforms the Python describer uses, and notes a layer that draws a column on another scale than an earlier layer of the same plot.</li><li><strong>Infinity matched every value.</strong> <code>abs(Inf - x) &lt;= 1e-9 * Inf</code> is true in R too; it mattered once transforms of 0 were compared. A value that is not finite matches only itself.</li><li><strong>A file shown before its cell ended stayed a picture.</strong> The figure view read a file&#39;s record once. It now asks again when the page&#39;s events move on; the board and the file view pass them.</li><li><strong>The tooltip broke at phone width.</strong> It was anchored beside the pointer with no room on either side.</li></ul><h2 id="also-observed" tabindex="-1">Also observed <a class="header-anchor" href="#also-observed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Also observed&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Each run ran shell commands in the panel&#39;s default mode, which the run&#39;s own settings did not allow, as in the earlier stages. In run 3 Claude Code ran its script with <code>Rscript</code>, and the corrected MA plot it wrote that way, <code>figures/ma_plot_colored.png</code>, is a picture. Both runs of the Python follow-up ran two shell commands as well.</li><li>In run 3 Claude Code overwrote a session variable the heatmap&#39;s cells had made (<code>top30</code>), said so in its answer and put it back.</li><li>The race check rewrote run 2&#39;s <code>ma_plot.png</code> with a plain <code>plotMA</code> and added <code>figures/race.png</code> to run 3&#39;s project; both are the person&#39;s files in disposable projects.</li><li>With <code>R_LIBS_USER</code> passed to the service, the board previewed CSV results (unlike <a href="/Rho/docs/records/python-figures-episode#also-observed">Python figures</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="follow-up-the-note-in-python" tabindex="-1">Follow-up: the note in Python <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-up-the-note-in-python" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Follow-up: the note in Python&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Figures 0.5.1 gives the Python describer the same note. The overlay check was then run in Python twice, once with the note and once without, to see what the note changes. A disposable project, <code>airway-overlay</code>, held the PyDESeq2 results of <a href="/Rho/docs/records/python-figures-episode">Python figures</a> run 4 (33,469 genes, <code>~ cell + dex</code>). The person drew run 2&#39;s mistake in a Python cell: every gene at <code>res.baseMean</code> in grey, then the 3,893 genes with an adjusted p below 0.05 at <code>np.log2(sig.baseMean)</code> in red, on a log x axis. They dragged over the upper red lobe (2,262 points: 1,120 red genes drawn at log2 of their mean and 1,142 grey genes whose mean is that low) and asked the question of run 3. Each run had a new service and project, with Claude Code 2.1.319 in its default mode through <code>workbuddy-ai/deepseek-v4.1-flash-sg</code>; Westlake&#39;s route again refused the third request of a four-request check.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures-files/py-overlay-note.png" alt="The overlay drawn in Python, with its note"></p><ul><li><strong>With the note</strong> (Figures 0.5.1), the red layer was bound to <code>res</code> as <code>x: log2(baseMean)</code> and the note showed under the figure. Claude Code answered in 73 s: <em>No, that&#39;s not right. It&#39;s a plotting bug in the cell, and the DESeq2 results are fine.</em> It quoted the two <code>scatter</code> calls, said the 1,142 grey points in the selection really are low-count genes and drew the corrected plot in a cell: the significant genes&#39; median mean is 553, against 7.3 for all genes.</li><li><strong>Without the note</strong>, the session loaded <code>py/figures.py</code> of Figures 0.5.0; its <code>figure.read</code> carried the same mapping and the cell&#39;s code, and no note. Claude Code answered in 82 s: <em>No, it isn&#39;t right. The plot draws the two layers on different scales, so the red points end up in the wrong place.</em> It quoted the same code and drew the same correction.</li></ul><p>Both answers came from the cell&#39;s code, which <code>figure.read</code> returns with the figure. Where the transform is written in the cell that draws, the note did not change the answer. One run of each does not measure how often either answer comes.</p><h2 id="follow-up-the-describer-checks-on-three-systems" tabindex="-1">Follow-up: the describer checks on three systems <a class="header-anchor" href="#follow-up-the-describer-checks-on-three-systems" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Follow-up: the describer checks on three systems&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Plugins figure describer checks had run only on this Mac, and CI did not run them. A temporary workflow on a <code>codex/</code> branch ran them for the selected Plugins on GitHub&#39;s macOS, Linux and Windows runners, with R 4.6.2, ggplot2 4.0.3, ComplexHeatmap, matplotlib 3.11.2 and seaborn 0.13.2 installed. A check skipped for a missing package fails the job.</p><ul><li><strong>First trial.</strong> The seaborn check failed on all three: <code>clustermap</code> needs scipy, which the workflow had not installed. On Linux and Windows every R check passed, file devices included.</li><li><strong>macOS.</strong> The check that leaves an <code>svg()</code> file open across cells failed: the next cell&#39;s plot was shown in the cell. A diagnostic cell printed the devices: <code>svg()</code> had opened no device at all. Plain R on that runner showed why: R&#39;s cairo module links XQuartz&#39;s libraries (<code>/opt/X11/lib/libXrender.1.dylib</code>), the runner has no XQuartz, and <code>svg()</code> warns <em>failed to load cairo DLL</em> and returns, while <code>capabilities(&quot;cairo&quot;)</code> still says <code>TRUE</code>. In the Notebook, the cell shows R&#39;s warning. This Mac has XQuartz.</li><li><strong>Last trial</strong>, with scipy and, on macOS, XQuartz: 12 of 12 checks passed on each system, none skipped, in 2.5 to 4 minutes per job.</li></ul><p>CI now runs this as the job <em>Figure describers</em> on the three systems; a person&#39;s Mac without XQuartz still cannot write <code>svg()</code> files, with or without Rho.</p><h2 id="not-established" tabindex="-1">Not established <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-established" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not established&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The Westlake route was not used; it still refused the third request.</li><li>A device opened through a copy a package imported before the session started (<code>importFrom</code>), or through ragg, svglite or Cairo directly, is not seen; its file stays a picture.</li><li>A PDF of several pages is not described. A device on a remote session is not watched.</li><li>Whether the note helps when the drawing cell&#39;s code does not show the transform (computed in an earlier cell or a helper function) is not established. The note compares layers bound to the same table only.</li><li>On a Mac without XQuartz, <code>svg()</code> and <code>cairo_pdf()</code> open no device, as R&#39;s own warning says; a person there gets no file to describe.</li><li>At phone width a heatmap&#39;s labels are still too small to read.</li></ul><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Plugins <code>npm run test:figures</code>: 12 of 12. The new check writes base graphics, a ggplot and a ComplexHeatmap to files with <code>png()</code>, <code>pdf()</code> and <code>svg()</code>, also inside a function, across cells and with numbered pages, refuses a two-page PDF, keeps a lattice page a picture, skips a file outside the project and adds no variables. The base graphics check binds a volcano drawn at <code>-log10(padj)</code> with a p-value of 0 and an MA overlay at <code>log2(baseMean)</code> with its note.</li><li>Plugins <code>npm test</code>: 211 of 214, 2 skipped; the one failure is the U1-06d check that failed before this change.</li><li>Rho <code>npm test</code>: 338 of 341, 3 skipped.</li><li>A local pack of Rho&#39;s selected plugins refused Plugins <code>7fd9c91</code>: a comment in the R describer named an analysis package, which the packer keeps out of runtime files. Plugins <code>aabeb66</code> rewords the comment, and the pack passed.</li><li>CI for <code>21ada47f</code> with Plugins <code>aabeb66</code> (run 42515375556): in the first attempt the Windows tests failed one continuity check. After the service was killed at <code>after-cli</code>, the restarted service found no Core Host (exit code 3, expected 0). This change does not touch that path, and no earlier failed run on <code>main</code> failed it. The rerun of that job on the same commit passed it, and all 15 jobs passed. Why the Host was missing once is not established.</li><li>Browser: the steps above in Chrome at 1440 and 390 px, read from their screenshots, and the race check with the previous and the new figure view.</li><li>Figures 0.5.1 (Plugins <code>1303108</code>): <code>npm run test:figures</code> 12 of 12; the matplotlib check binds significant genes drawn at <code>np.log2</code> of their mean over an MA plot on a log axis and expects the note in the record and the cell&#39;s plot output. Plugins <code>npm test</code> 211 of 214, 2 skipped, the same U1-06d failure. Rho <code>npm test</code> first failed one check, which gives a plugin 2 s, under the full suite&#39;s load (<code>extensions.test.mjs</code>, 3 of 3 alone); the next full run passed 338 of 341, 3 skipped. All 15 CI jobs passed for <code>8a2f3b99</code> (run 42522637879).</li><li>Describer checks on GitHub&#39;s runners, branch <code>codex/describer-checks</code>: runs 42522863869 (scipy missing everywhere; <code>svg()</code> on macOS), 42523544383 and 42523983893 (the devices and plain R on macOS), and 42524416843 (12 of 12 on each system, none skipped). In CI, all 18 jobs passed for <code>2ddbddc2</code> (run 42525078013), the three Figure describers jobs among them.</li></ul>',47)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
